{"summary":"Reject all three amendments. The save/load determinism failure is real, but the proposed habitat-registry diagnosis is not supported by the code path; the other two amendments describe issues that the current nitrogen-cycle/tracing API already addresses.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-4-4f43dda9","decision":"reject","rationale":"`save_load_with_active_cycle_state` does fail, but the cited root cause is wrong. Reproducing the load boundary shows the immediate post-load drift is in carbonate cache fields (`water.ph`, `water.bicarbonate_mg_total`), triggered by `normalize_loaded_carbonate_state()` in `save.rs`, not by `state.refresh_habitat_registry()` in `Engine::from_parts`. A fix that skips habitat refresh on load would not restore bit-identical continuation and risks breaking the intentional stale-registry repair path."},{"amendment_id":"fr-4-d53b17ff","decision":"reject","rationale":"The inconsistency described is already fixed in the current code. `NitrogenCycleOutput` now separates `nitrate_produced_mg_n` from `tan_oxidized_mg`, and `engine.rs` records both alongside `alkalinity_consumed_meq`. That means NOB-only ticks can report nitrate production without implying TAN oxidation, and alkalinity attribution remains tied to the TAN-oxidation basis."},{"amendment_id":"fr-4-a31d97c5","decision":"reject","rationale":"The bookkeeping surface is not one-sided anymore. `NitrogenCycleOutput` exposes both `alkalinity_consumed_meq` and `alkalinity_produced_meq`, and `engine.rs` publishes both as stage metrics/trace notes in addition to the net pool delta. If denitrification is added inside the same stage later, gross consumption and gross production are already separately attributable without another API redesign."}]}